// src/v4/extdeps/languages/machine_code.dag
//
// Scope: machine code model — direction-agnostic, the BOTTOM of the
//        abstraction stack. emit: Node -> encoded instructions; ingest:
//        bytes -> Node (disassembly). PARAMETERIZED BY AN ISA model:
//        one machine_code model, the ISA is the parameter, exactly as
//        `LanguageModel` parameterizes the B2-OMNI boundary
//        (fractal/consistent; O(N+M), not one file per ISA).
// Anchor: the ISA specification is the (parameterized) versioned fidelity
//         authority (L-2) — Intel 64 & IA-32 Architectures SDM /
//         Arm Architecture Reference Manual (Arm ARM); pin a revision.
// Stress (T-4.13 — a B2-OMNI falsification probe): the extreme of every
//   boundary. (a) No cosmetic surface at all — F is purely instruction
//   encoding; the strongest test that C5-fidelity holds at the limit.
//   (b) Disassembly is ingest-is-harder taken to the extreme: MOST byte
//   sequences are not valid/meaningful instructions → the fail-closed
//   boundary is enormous. A disassembler that "guesses" is the no-engine
//   violation made visible — this probe forces fail-closed honesty.
// Clear win: `.dag → machine code` (we don't *necessarily* output a
//   binary — we CAN), and `bytes → Node` disassembly, both via one
//   ISA-parameterized model.
//
// Shape (operator-ratified L-3): ONE `machine_code.dag` parameterized by
//   an `Isa` model. Per-ISA files (`x86_64.dag`, `arm64.dag`, …) would
//   reintroduce the N×M trap B2-OMNI forbids.
//
// Owns:
//   - machine_code grammar parameterized by `Isa` (declarative,
//     bidirectional): instruction encoding/decoding, registers, operands
//   - disassembly ingest is fail-closed: a byte run that does not decode
//     to a valid instruction is a Diagnostic, NEVER a fabricated/"best
//     effort" instruction (no-engine, C5)
//   - Cost realization: per-instruction cost on the parameterized ISA
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Node pivot + behavior substrate
//   - std/diagnostic.dag: fail-closed ingest/disassembly diagnostics
//   - std/machine.dag: Byte, Word*, MachineWidth, PointerWidth
//   - extdeps/languages/* canonical LanguageModel shape from T-4
//
// Prerequisite correction (T-4.13): this file is NOT fillable after T-1
// alone. The worker must wait for std/machine.dag (T-3 scalar/numeric
// stack: logic + nat -> machine) and T-4's canonical LanguageModel-as-data
// shape. Do not invent local Byte/Word carriers or a private language model
// schema here.
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-4.13

module v4.extdeps.languages.machine_code
